# =============================================================================
# numerus<d32> — decimal arithmetic at the scaled carrier
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Annotated decimal literals — `numerus<d32> a ← 4.2` carries the exact
#     digit text to typing; 4.2 is 42×10⁻¹, not a float approximation.
#   • Scaled-integer execution — arithmetic runs on `Value::Int` carriers
#     (d32 = 10⁷, d64 = 10⁹) with checked 128-bit intermediates and
#     round-half-even reductions, so 4.2 + 0.1 = 4.3 exactly.
#   • The runner prints the RAW scaled carrier — 4.2 prints as `42000000`
#     (42 × 10⁶ at the d32 scale), and 4.2 + 0.1 prints `43000000`
#     (43 × 10⁶). Exactness is visible in the carrier, not in a formatted
#     decimal string.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • reading a printed carrier as a formatted decimal — `43000000` IS 4.3
#     at the d32 scale, not forty-three million
#   • assuming decimal arithmetic degrades to float error — the scaled
#     integer carrier keeps 4.2 + 0.1 = 4.3 exact where f64 rounds
#   • treating integer literals as decimal literals — `a + 1`, `a / 2`, and
#     `numerus<d32> e ← 5` reject with `decimal_integer_literal_rejected`;
#     write `1.0`, `2.0`, or `5.0`, or use explicit `↦` conversion
#
# See also: numerus, d64, ↦, fractus
# =============================================================================

# decimal — 4.2 arithmetic at the d32 carrier
#
# WHY: pins the D4 runner contract end-to-end. Each line below prints the
# raw scaled carrier of the expression to its right.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   decimal-arithmetic.expected — raw d32/d64 carriers, one per print.

main {
    const numerus<d32> a ← 4.2
    const numerus<d32> b ← 0.1

    print a
    print b
    print a + b
    print a - b
    print a * b
    print a / b

    const numerus<d64> x ← 4.2
    const numerus<d64> y ← 0.1

    print x + y
    print x * y
}
